cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software, please cite it as below."
title: "ACC-Optimization"
type: software
version: "1.0.0-srep"
license: "GPL-3.0-only"
abstract: "This study compares five metaheuristic algorithms—Genetic Algorithm (GA), Particle Swarm Optimization (PSO), Komodo Mlipir Algorithm (KMA), Stochastic Komodo Algorithm (SKA), and Hiking Optimization Algorithm (HOA)—for tuning control parameters in an Adaptive Cruise Control (ACC) system. The evaluation is conducted using four objective functions, namely IAE, ISE, ITAE, and ITSE, with 30 independent runs and 300 iterations per run. Performance is assessed in terms of optimization solution quality, inter-run stability, convergence behavior, computational efficiency, and control performance validation under cruise control and car-following scenarios based on time-to-collision (TTC). Compared with GA, PSO provides the most consistent improvement in solution quality, achieving average fitness reductions of approximately 1.1% to 5.1% across all objective functions. KMA and SKA exhibit comparable improvements to PSO, while HOA yields more moderate gains, ranging from approximately 1.0% to 2.7%. In terms of stability, PSO, KMA, and SKA significantly reduce the dispersion of results, with interquartile range (IQR) reductions approaching 100% relative to GA across all objectives. HOA demonstrates strong stability improvements for certain objectives but does not consistently outperform GA across all metrics. Regarding computational efficiency, GA is the fastest algorithm. Relative to GA, the average runtime overhead per run is approximately 1.1% to 1.5% for PSO, 2.1% to 2.6% for KMA, 0.3% to 0.5% for HOA, and approximately 139% for SKA. In control performance validation, all optimized controllers exhibit stable responses without overshoot. PSO, KMA, and SKA achieve lower steady-state errors than GA, with improvements ranging from approximately 0.3% to 12%, depending on the objective function. In the robustness evaluation across 12 different scenarios, the control system remains stable without overshoot. IIn cruise control mode, PSO, KMA, and SKA form the leading group, whereas GA exhibits the largest steady-state-error degradation, reaching 11.45% for IAE/ITAE, 1.64% for ISE, and 0.23% for ITSE relative to that group. In car-following scenarios, all methods ensure safe operation, as the minimum TTC value ($\approx$ 17 s) remains well above the critical threshold of 1.5 s."
